    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel frustrated and to question the process after putting significant effort into two rounds of interviews for a leadership role, especially when you have deep technical tenure and have received no feedback after two weeks.\n\nHowever, on the question of whether you should make something of the fact that the panel was all women: **in practical terms, no.** Basing any challenge or assumption on the panel’s gender composition is unlikely to be productive and carries significant professional risk.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why, along with an objective look at what might actually have driven the decision and how best to proceed.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-female panel indicate bias?\n\nOn its own, **no**. Just as an all-male interview panel hiring a male candidate does not automatically prove gender discrimination, an all-female panel hiring a woman is not proof of bias. \n\nIn many engineering consultancies, interview panels for group head roles are drawn from specific functions—such as the regional managing director, an HR/people lead, an operations director, and an adjacent discipline head. If those roles happen to be held by women, the panel reflects the leadership structure rather than an intent to favor a particular gender. \n\nLegally and procedurally, demonstrating unlawful discrimination requires evidence that the decision was influenced by gender rather than job-related competencies. The demographic makeup of the panel and the successful candidate is circumstantial and would not stand on its own as evidence of bias.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Leadership roles vs. technical tenure\n\nWhile your technical credentials (15 years, master’s degree, 3 major project leads) are strong, the position was **Head of the Structural Engineering Group**. \n\nAt this tier, selection criteria often shift significantly away from technical execution and heavily toward:\n* **Business development and revenue generation:** The announcement specifically highlighted her *\"excellent client relationships.\"* In consulting, winning work, retaining high-value clients, and cross-selling disciplines are often prioritized over years of calculation and design experience.\n* **People leadership and culture:** Managing team retention, mentoring junior staff, and resolving internal conflict.\n* **Strategic fit and interview performance:** How well the candidate articulated a 3- to 5-year vision for the team, fee structures, and market expansion.\n\nWith a chartered license and eight years of experience, the successful candidate met the baseline professional standard. If her commercial network, client relationships, or interview presentation aligned more closely with what the consultancy currently needs, that would fully explain the hiring decision without any bias involved.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why haven't they responded about feedback?\n\nTwo weeks without written feedback is frustrating, but it is standard in many corporate environments:\n* **HR slow-turnaround:** Drafting tailored, written candidate feedback requires sign-off from busy panel members and HR.\n* **Legal caution:** Many firms have strict policies against providing detailed written feedback to senior candidates to minimize liability, often preferring a brief phone call or standard template.\n* **Onboarding priorities:** The hiring team's attention typically shifts immediately to finalizing the offer and contract with the preferred candidate.\n\n---\n\n### 4. The risks of raising gender\n\nIf you decide to \"make something\" of the panel's gender—either formally or informally:\n* **Defensive reaction:** Accusing the panel or the firm of gender-based favoritism without direct evidence will immediately close the door to honest feedback or future opportunities at that firm.\n* **Reputational risk:** Senior structural engineering communities are relatively small. Raising claims of bias based solely on the gender of the panel and candidate can quickly harm your professional standing in the wider market.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow up politely on the feedback request:**\n   Send a short, professional nudge focusing purely on your own professional growth. For example:\n   > *\"I wanted to follow up on my note from two weeks ago. I appreciated the opportunity to meet the panel and learn more about the team's direction. As I continue evaluating leadership opportunities, I would greatly value any high-level feedback the panel might have on areas where I can further align my profile with roles of this scope—particularly regarding commercial strategy and client development. Even a brief 10-minute call would be appreciated.\"*\n\n2. **Offer a phone call instead of a written document:**\n   Panel members are far more candid over the phone than in writing, where they fear creating legal exposure.\n\n3. **Reflect on the interview discussions:**\n   Consider what questions were emphasized during the two rounds. Were they focused on technical delivery and risk management, or on client acquisition, fees, and leadership vision? This may give you immediate clarity on where the other candidate scored higher.\n",
